1043 #edb/ref 17592186134855 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134016 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134016 #edb/ref 1045 "The Divergence of a Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134016 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134817 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134016 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049465 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134016 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1faaba32-288c-58f7-9e8b-36ad43309761" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407662 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105555" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134071 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db7680f4-57f3-5a1a-a81a-52c92b75f829" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134071 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134855 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1061 32111 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c2a161a-d267-5999-8795-ba9baba0146f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134817 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135108 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134850 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1061 32553 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d2f80d4-10e2-5fdc-b52f-7b5cadac99d2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134832 #edb/ref 1061 33322 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134832 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb39b82a-9c43-56ff-9c10-70ad9f735fab" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134832 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133886 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134854 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1061 34359 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a9298c4-106a-569c-bd0b-5d24d3e71b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135120 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134848 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1061 35525 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77b5a013-eab6-50c3-9f63-2a51f2e967b2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133895 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134854 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134832 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134854 #edb/ref 1061 35536 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134854 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88b27d23-7358-51e8-9457-a8b063ed5a52" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134854 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135126 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134855 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134828 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134855 #edb/ref 1061 35537 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134855 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e856caa2-317e-5779-b004-597af558a506" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186134855 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135127 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135108 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ exist, then the **divergence** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is the scalar given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as \n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the dot product\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\, \\mathbf i + e^{yx} \\, \\mathbf j .\n$$\n\nThen, we can calculate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the dot product formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =(i\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}+j\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y})\\cdot (2xy\\,i+{e}^{yx}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yx}) \\\\  & =2y+x{e}^{yx}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135108 #edb/ref 1065 5116 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135108 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "086f12c3-0ab5-54ed-8b4e-5c70e8c7c7e5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135108 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1064 "Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the divergence of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ and the point $S,$ shown below. Imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water.\n\n![](images/a7/a785c84976242d699af11ab2203600e2007e95e35b60665ba9882c8f0f25bce3.png)\n\nWe can see from our diagram that $S$ acts like a **source**. You can think of this as an open faucet, pushing water onto the plane and away from itself.\n\nSince $S$ acts like a source, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is nonzero. By convention, if a point $S$ acts like a source, the divergence is positive: $$\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$$ In this particular case, *every* point in our vector field acts as a source. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$ everywhere.\n\nIf we reverse the situation, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b4/b4c923bbde36568e4e2f36f7d388622a520d0799d558049f72d0194e0bd679f7.png)\n\nWe can see from our diagram that $T$ acts like a **sink**. You can think of this as an open plughole that sucks in the surrounding water.\n\nSince $T$ acts like a sink, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is also nonzero. By convention, if a point $T$ acts like a sink, the divergence is negative: $$\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$$ Every point in our vector field acts like a sink in this case. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$ everywhere.\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of some vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **solenoidal.**" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1065 5518 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a2cdf73b-116c-5cf4-a5a2-a8c95e447127" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135120 #edb/ref 1067 "Physical Interpretation of Divergence" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135126 #edb/ref 1064 "The following two vector fields behave either as a source or a sink. Therefore, they both have divergence.\n\n![](images/93/939efa85ebfe4805a27595b60a797b8a3435075590ee77f7a18d04bd76d91f1a.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are solenoidal (i.e., they each have zero divergence).\n\n![](images/ed/edb2f908fa78439bf985b5754c9fcc5968c21926dbe124297b67af046a9f70a3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135126 #edb/ref 1065 5793 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddbe7ae2-caaf-5436-b1c7-aabc0eec115d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135126 #edb/ref 1067 "Examples of Vector Field With Divergence and Solenoidal Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1064 "There are a few things we should note about the divergence:\n\n- We can only compute the divergence of a *vector* field, and a vector field's divergence is a *scalar* field. So if $G(x,y) = \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,G(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{scalar field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{div}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\n- We can easily extend the notion of divergence to three dimensions. So, if $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k,$ then $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z},\n$$\n\nwhere the three-dimensional differential operator $\\nabla$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n- The divergence of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the tendency of the vector field to behave like a source or a sink at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1065 5794 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9b8de083-51c2-581b-ad36-a773dca8af05" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Important Notes About the Divergence of a Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407665 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407666 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407667 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1045 "The Divergence of a Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186407666 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046665 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049465 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407644 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "23bb149c-27d7-5070-88b1-a3124f094ad2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407645 #edb/ref 1081 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407645 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407645 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "03e74b99-ee56-54e9-ae3f-42d5ee7c0a33" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407646 #edb/ref 1081 "2-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407646 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407646 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "08e054f8-7842-51d9-944e-506f086c64ff" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407647 #edb/ref 1081 "\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407647 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407647 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "30bd69d5-9aed-5f53-bd57-88c0d8b71e93" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407648 #edb/ref 1081 "I and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407648 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407648 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "5070438f-bcf7-51c2-8463-01a5c462b9d7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407649 #edb/ref 1081 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407649 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407649 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "67f8c8f5-44cd-5c0e-a53a-65de21b3fa5a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407650 #edb/ref 1081 "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407650 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407650 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407651 #edb/ref 1081 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407651 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407651 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "803f938a-69cc-5cc9-9644-a563d2a7cf5f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407652 #edb/ref 1081 "\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407652 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407652 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "941fb884-9dbd-5e6e-bdb3-9c13326a2f3e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407653 #edb/ref 1081 "-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407653 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407653 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "951b5e1a-06b7-5f54-b81b-2e7935528e41" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407654 #edb/ref 1081 "3-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407654 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407654 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "b2537554-7ead-5776-b7ea-b0a5c6ea2d72" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407655 #edb/ref 1081 "III and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407655 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407655 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "c3b2e55e-a370-570d-ba5a-e8f0f307eabf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407656 #edb/ref 1081 "\\frac{1}{xy}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{z}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407656 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407656 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "d9966256-2b26-5637-969d-ec652f55ff58" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407657 #edb/ref 1081 "1+\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407657 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407657 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "de576d13-a320-57ea-81fe-65cb1f522e77" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407658 #edb/ref 1081 "\\frac{1}{xy}\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407658 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407658 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "ec0916bb-4bf2-5bfb-8625-f74c2a28d4bf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407659 #edb/ref 1081 "\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407659 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407659 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "eecb625e-fb38-58c5-8c7d-425f0374e2c6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407646 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407647 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407653 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407654 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407657 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407646 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407660 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "55f12593-79dd-5e1b-8574-a974a94bfc5a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407645 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407648 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407649 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407651 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407655 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407645 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407661 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "f0ff6b8b-be06-5d38-bc53-8c8fbff6866c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407650 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407652 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407656 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407658 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407659 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407650 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407662 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "fafbd1ee-fb02-5785-b3f4-4426b308c48e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407661 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105572" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407663 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/58/584016c88e5916984e181118d52caab95dba1fd0d861e0fd248a4e023d1b07da.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2847820b-e13e-563d-bfbb-bab640514e5b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407663 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move horizontally and vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407664 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407660 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407664 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105559" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407664 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407664 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,j+{e}^{2z}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407664 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f48c368c-417f-5ffe-9791-056b549ac545" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407664 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2z}) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y+\\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y+2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(0,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,1,0) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0-\\pi +2 \\\\  & =2-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407665 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407667 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407665 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d73206f-cfb2-5389-b819-954a52ec5c0a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407665 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407664 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407666 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407665 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43f42226-1025-58c1-878e-7c4041f020a2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134071 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407667 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84dd7364-713d-5340-9f9c-2da553617182" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407667 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407663 #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
